<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Latest news reports on FRANCE, French politics and culture - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">Follow FRANCE 24’s coverage of events in Paris and across France, Corsica and the French overseas territories. An insider look at French politics, culture, society, technology and sport through on-the-ground reports, investigations, exclusive interviews, debates and analyses.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/france/rss</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:58Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Paris neighbourhoods: Exploring the lesser-known side of the 18th arrondissement</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3ea1019fee2bcf4189c39c36cb245c6182114bb780bf3d698cc3ad5f273513bb</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:25Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T11:30:47Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/french-connections/20261029-paris-neighbourhoods-exploring-the-lesser-known-side-of-the-18th-arrondissement--14cc25ce38de.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/french-connections/20261029-paris-neighbourhoods-exploring-the-lesser-known-side-of-the-18th-arrondissement" /><summary type="html">In this edition of French Connections, Genie Godula and Florence Villeminot continue their tour of Paris with a stop in the 18th arrondissement. The district is famous for landmarks like the Sacré-Cœur Basilica and the Moulin Rouge, but in this show we step away from the picture-postcard version of Montmartre and take you to see the rest of the 18th. We visit the vibrant multicultural neighbourhood called the Goutte d'Or, then take you to the largest fabric store in the world and introduce you to some of the 18th's most famous residents over the years. Join us as we explore the lesser-known side of the 18th arrondissement.</summary><category term="French connections" /></entry><entry><title>Paris shooting suspect admitted to targeting Muslims and Africans, prosecutor says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:093575b628e5679e32d4865b6651eb0007b333f60f5159a6b435eb0c608079f1</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T09:48:58Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261029-live-french-anti-terrorism-prosecutor-to-give-press-conference-on-racist-paris-shooting--2b30237198b2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261029-live-french-anti-terrorism-prosecutor-to-give-press-conference-on-racist-paris-shooting" /><summary type="html">A shooter suspected of killing one person and wounding five others, including a child, last week in the town of Trappes, near Paris, admitted to targeting Muslims and Africans, France’s anti-terrorism prosecutor said. The 39-year-old is set to be held in pre-trial detention and will appear before judges on Thursday.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>Mbappé's China faux pas: When the sports world collides with the complexities of geopolitics</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1bc03656e2c06153f0657591b0205b138d7873041323adc12a1058c8e5cd3755</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:46Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T12:17:34Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-mbapp_-s-china-faux-pas-when-the-sports-world-collides-with-the-complexities-of-geopolitics--54f990e8a7f4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261028-mbapp%C3%A9-s-china-faux-pas-when-the-sports-world-collides-with-the-complexities-of-geopolitics" /><summary type="html">François Picard welcomes Igor Mladenovic, a French-Serbian sports writer, sports business analyst and former football commentator based in Luxembourg. For Mladenovic, the backlash in China over Kylian Mbappé's playful reference to a meme depicting him in the likeness of Mao Zedong illustrates a dilemma of the digital age: how a seemingly innocuous anecdote from a football superstar can escalate into a geopolitical firestorm.</summary></entry><entry><title>French Senate backs 'make rioters pay' bill despite rights concerns</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:85cc8c65e494762126c89a24948d4d93b01a5bde1488e67819171c43779458be</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:49Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T00:10:35Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261028-french-senate-backs-bill-to-make-protesters-pay-for-unrest-damage-despite-rights-concern--c0c569a98c90.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261028-french-senate-backs-bill-to-make-protesters-pay-for-unrest-damage-despite-rights-concern" /><summary type="html">France's Senate on Wednesday approved a bill authorising rioters to be held financially accountable for damage caused during social unrest, with penalties of up to €10,000 and possible welfare benefit seizures. Rights groups warn the legislation could undermine the public's fundamental right to protest.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>Rights groups say proposed French law to make rioters pay for damage is anti-democratic</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:52b4a8ba12edb7d75bc6db8c218e353a7ebf4f676ab805d462f5c1d40f592e37</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:52Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T18:27:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261027-rights-groups-say-proposed-french-law-to-make-rioters-pay-for-damage-anti-democratic--4c29f18dbef7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261027-rights-groups-say-proposed-french-law-to-make-rioters-pay-for-damage-anti-democratic" /><summary type="html">Critics have said that a proposed French law that would make rioters liable to pay thousands of euros for any damage they cause is anti-democratic, as it could undermine the legitimate right to protest. The government put forward the law in a bid to quell looting and vandalism on the fringes of ongoing national protests over poor conditions in French schools and the high cost of living.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: Lawmakers hold a minute of silence for Pengo Bossio Wangi, victim of racist attack</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9d2776e6c020f5d1bf62a6a145966e7cdcb995f0ed70fefb6962c36a4d900ab3</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:33Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T17:08:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-france-lawmakers-hold-a-minute-of-silence-for-pengo-bossio-wangi-victim-of-racist-attack-1--d1f12ab1013c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261027-france-lawmakers-hold-a-minute-of-silence-for-pengo-bossio-wangi-victim-of-racist-attack-1" /><summary type="html">French lawmakers held a minute of silence for Pengo Bossio Wangi. The victim of a racist attack died in a shooting last week, while at least five other people were wounded, including a 12-years-old girl. FRANCE 24’s Claire Paccalin reports from the ground.</summary></entry><entry><title>Revisiting the delicate art of French patisserie</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:58602ffc2c17a42f613033ebac4ba7c72e00d55a8955e3f05ceb3b4ecdc93b28</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T13:45:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/entre-nous/20261027-revisiting-the-delicate-art-of-french-patisserie--0adc80aacb3f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/entre-nous/20261027-revisiting-the-delicate-art-of-french-patisserie" /><summary type="html">In this edition of Entre Nous, we meet Nour Kandler, a self-taught pastry chef whose approach to French patisserie is shaped by a deep appreciation for its traditions and the rich flavours of her Middle Eastern heritage. She shares her unique perspective on pastry, where classic French techniques intertwine with cultural influences to create a style that is both personal and distinctive.</summary><category term="Entre Nous" /></entry><entry><title>French government concessions to students 'tactically shrewd' but risk creating new set of problems</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1e98cfdcc61fcde4809d8bef33ebf5be21c120747f9bfce60b022a143768d825</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:20:58Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T11:35:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/french-government-concessions-to-students-tactically-shrewd-but-risk-creating-new-set-of-problems--306b230dc2d0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-government-concessions-to-students-tactically-shrewd-but-risk-creating-new-set-of-problems" /><summary type="html">Oliver Farry welcomes Oliver Davis, Professor and Head of the Department of French at University College Cork. According to Professor Davis, the government's handling of the student protests reflects a combination of "tactical confusion" and an inadequate response to deeper structural problems. Davis cautions the concessions offered to secondary school students merely "scratch the surface." Meanwhile, France's widening protest movement has extended well beyond the classroom, drawing in the Yellow Vests, trade unions and people from across French society, amid mounting frustrations over public services and the rising cost of living.</summary></entry><entry><title>French education reform proposals 'vague': Govt created 'most of the problems' it now seeks to solve</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3c0c9c57d60b7ae26694fec4eedbe619911239c00c99433f3c97866f75cb31d3</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:02Z</updated><published>2026-10-27T07:31:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261027-french-education-reform-proposals-vague-govt-created-most-of-the-problems-it-now-seek-to-so--9099e1389e64.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261027-french-education-reform-proposals-vague-govt-created-most-of-the-problems-it-now-seek-to-solve" /><summary type="html">François Picard welcomes Jean-Rémi Girard, president of the SNALC teachers' union, who places responsibility for France's educational difficulties not simply on demographic change or fiscal constraints, but on years of political decisions that, he argues, have weakened the state education system. Against a backdrop of student protests, budgetary uncertainty and mounting concerns over educational inequality, Girard contends that the French government faces the formidable task of addressing a crisis largely of its own making.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>France protests: Macron rules out state of emergency – for now</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5770affc289ad737db619ef342810507a4de8531abb44dfa0f6b6f9d80683c84</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:04Z</updated><published>2026-10-26T16:16:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-france-protests-macron-rules-out-state-of-emergency-_-for-now--d78e2f54223a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-france-protests-macron-rules-out-state-of-emergency-%E2%80%93-for-now" /><summary type="html">The French government sought to draw a line Monday under weeks of protests that have been marred by violence, with President Emmanuel Macron heading to Marseille. France has been rocked by widespread anger over the rising cost of living, with nationwide demonstrations on October 17 degenerating into some of the country's worst rioting in years.</summary></entry><entry><title>Macron promises 'concrete measures' in response to French students' demands</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b2bbc0dca70b2ab8165910181af095c647ca563a004dbc70688a8bb08b10ba2d</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:33Z</updated><published>2026-10-26T14:39:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-french-president-promises-concrete-measures-in-response-to-french-students-demands--507afbcad5c6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261026-french-president-promises-concrete-measures-in-response-to-french-students-demands" /><summary type="html">French President Emmanuel Macron said the government would announce “concrete measures" on Monday (October 26) to address school students’ demands after weeks of unrest. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu was scheduled to meet with students later Monday. FRANCE 24's Luke Shrago reports from the ground.</summary></entry><entry><title>French student representatives hail govt's ‘concrete measures’, call for protests to continue</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fdb24ba1f08043214fe7e2a6934c428a7086e82ffe04c0d75b43d58d112df943</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:07Z</updated><published>2026-10-26T12:47:12Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-live-france-will-maintain-order-without-state-of-emergency-but-it-s-not-off-the-table-macro--b476afc7ab24.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-live-france-will-maintain-order-without-state-of-emergency-but-it-s-not-off-the-table-macron-says" /><summary type="html">Representatives from French high school student groups on Monday hailed the government’s first measures to calm the crisis over ongoing unrest linked to their protest movement, including a gradual replacement of higher education admissions platform Parcoursup, but called for “continued mobilisation” after their meeting with Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu. Read about the day’s events as they unfolded on our liveblog.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>French students vow to continue protests despite concessions from Macron govt</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:def2d6a32fcee12eae48ffb0d3c17fa10ef5beffaa18f18377dba839ea35d3cd</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:18:33Z</updated><published>2026-10-26T12:25:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-macron-vows-concrete-measures-to-address-student-demands-after-unrest--db472c08be22.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261026-macron-vows-concrete-measures-to-address-student-demands-after-unrest" /><summary type="html">French student leaders on Monday called for more protests despite President Emmanuel Macron's pledge of  "concrete and strong" measures to address their demands after weeks of nationwide unrest. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu met with high school representatives alongside education ministers to announce follow-up action ranging from a total overhaul of higher education admissions platform Parcoursup to a cap on class sizes.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>French PM meets student representatives after weeks of protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:eddcef0efabc84b41992e881792df0b861c2bec895cadda5bae92c9eff8eda37</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:10Z</updated><published>2026-10-26T12:20:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/french-pm-meets-student-representatives-after-weeks-of-protests--2d535383d1ec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-pm-meets-student-representatives-after-weeks-of-protests" /><summary type="html">French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu is set to meet with high school students on Monday afternoon (October 26) to ease tensions in the country after nearly a month of protests. This follows another meeting in which French president Emmanuel Macron promised a concrete response to demands for better education standards and funding.</summary></entry><entry><title>Marine Le Pen's 'Renaissance': Fourth time lucky?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ee8a82ecdecaa64c5d8763cbb07983f85bd06d39e0d6e97eb712adbf1fcdaf28</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:14Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T19:05:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261025-le-pen-s-renaissance-4th-time-lucky--5f1d39fb10e3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261025-le-pen-s-renaissance-4th-time-lucky" /><summary type="html">Just before half past four this Sunday, Marine Le Pen took to the stage under the stadium strobe lights at the Zénith Arena in Orléans. The far-right French politician, who analysts say is on the doorstep of the presidency, is a polarising figure yet increasingly popular with her base, and has topped the popularity polls for months in France. Her entrance came 24 hours after the latest protests across the country, which included over 10,000 anti-Le Pen demonstrators outside the venue on Saturday.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>France: Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:94579e9d4b20ac5922629651f4103b9d39111aa1cb42875c87e05aafc8a42ffb</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:16Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T17:12:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off--ddfcca28f719.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off" /><summary type="html">Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest. Lecturer in Liberal Arts at Queen Mary University of London, Andrew Smith, shares further analysis.</summary></entry><entry><title>French far-right leader Le Pen kicks off presidential campaign</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f555ed2536610e9406fdfb93ac59873f24285b2b4250581a8d19a468aee9567d</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:19Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T16:38:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/french-far-right-leader-le-pen-kicks-off-presidential-campaign--ab902b055a14.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/french-far-right-leader-le-pen-kicks-off-presidential-campaign" /><summary type="html">We’ve just been listening to Marine Le Pen, leader of the far-right National Rally and its presidential candidate, delivering her first official campaign speech in Orléans.

She spoke of what she called a “renaissance” for France and the dawn of a new era. She also condemned what she described as “Islamist ideologies” that promote violence, saying they are incompatible with France.

Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan was listening in Orléans and joins us now with more.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: 1,314 arrested during protests across the country</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c85e4ae63066571acfd0453fafec9feb27fb5acbc96b50d6c7f3b6b57a7b95a1</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:22Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T16:32:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-1-314-arrested-during-protests-across-the-country--fc20118c2df1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-1-314-arrested-during-protests-across-the-country" /><summary type="html">France saw another day of nationwide protests on Saturday, with more than 400,000 people taking to the streets over the rising cost of living and school funding. Most marches remained peaceful, a week after riots and looting hit several cities. However, police say more than 1,300 people were arrested nationwide as violence broke out on the sidelines of demonstrations. Caroline Baum has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>French far-right leader Le Pen seeks to leverage unrest in presidential campaign kick-off</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1f14bd2bfa35054855e8d6373cf88117b0de3d6565e974f95a5446dbaab4c563</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:26Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T15:01:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-french-far-right-leader-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off--8f8e11bfae3f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-french-far-right-leader-le-pen-seeks-to-leverage-unrest-in-presidential-campaign-kick-off" /><summary type="html">Marine Le Pen, leader of France’s far-right National Rally, addressed some 10,000 supporters on Sunday in the city of Orleans as she kicked off her presidential campaign, a day after nationwide cost-of-living protests flared again into violence. Le Pen, the frontrunner, is seen to be benefiting in the polls from the weeks of unrest.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: 110,000 protesters in Paris</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a917f8b482e579df8c7c0e6580874e98bd5791a159735825b3898dca8880f144</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T15:00:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-110-000-protesters-in-paris--08d0963b371d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-110-000-protesters-in-paris" /><summary type="html">Hundreds of demonstrations took place across France on Saturday over the rising cost of living, with security forces deployed in large numbers to prevent a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. While the marches themselves “passed off well” in several cities, Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez said members of “ultra-left” groups threw projectiles and fireworks at police in an attempt to provoke clashes. French Communist Party spokesperson Fatima Khallouk shares more details.</summary></entry><entry><title>Calais, 10 years after the Jungle: Migrants still risk the Channel crossing</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cc89ef8836cea95dab94f9be6af8e1a26972ff40823b31217d5c97d4c6fd0bd9</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:31Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T14:17:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-calais-10-years-after-the-jungle-migrants-still-risk-the-channel-crossing--d127e5ff3783.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261025-calais-10-years-after-the-jungle-migrants-still-risk-the-channel-crossing" /><summary type="html">Ten years ago, in 2016, thousands of #migrants lived in what was known as the “Great #Jungle”, a vast camp on the outskirts of #Calais. It took the authorities seven days to dismantle it.
 
Today, dozens of smaller “jungles” are scattered across a wider stretch of the coastline. These camps are dismantled every 48 hours on average.
 
For migrants, the goal remains the same: to cross the Channel and reach the United Kingdom. Despite the risks posed by “small boats”, these makeshift vessels, many continue to attempt the crossing in the hope of a better life.
 
Our reporters Pauline Godart, Claire Paccalin and Mohamed Farhat travelled to northern France for #BilletRetour.</summary></entry><entry><title>Le Pen leads polls with up to 38% six months before election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:574255e2fb3ccab5d8619df2cf3cc9743a6ae097da56b647b3960f879b2f5960</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:34Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T13:51:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/le-pen-leads-polls-with-up-to-38-six-months-before-election--ac3f248775c5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/le-pen-leads-polls-with-up-to-38-six-months-before-election" /><summary type="html">The day after the first day of the National Rally congress in Orléans, in central France, passed without any major incidents, Marine Le Pen is due to deliver her first major campaign speech on Sunday afternoon, 25 October, under heavy police surveillance. The speech, centred on the theme of renewal, aims to inject a dose of optimism six months ahead of the presidential election, the candidate has reportedly said in private.
 
Gilles Ivaldi, a political science researcher at the French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS) and the University of Nice, explains that the violence following protests across France in recent weeks could help the National Rally present itself as the party capable of restoring public safety and national security.</summary></entry><entry><title>France: Le Pen launches presidential campaign in Orleans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:79d0c74d69cee6c98b199edd562b26e23ed995f89312d02cc40852ee64dde355</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:37Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T13:46:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-launches-presidential-campaign-in-orleans--72c4510f4dbf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-le-pen-launches-presidential-campaign-in-orleans" /><summary type="html">Far-right leader Marine Le Pen is launching her presidential campaign today. The National Rally candidate is due to deliver her first campaign speech in Orléans, addressing around 10,000 party members. Police were controlling traffic entering the city this morning, following a large anti-far-right demonstration on Saturday, where thousands of protesters marched chanting anti-fascist slogans.</summary></entry><entry><title>Far-right leader Le Pen says visible policing keeps protests smooth</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:609bea7eb528ac385c23eeb013129b4435c1795d53cee10b24a252d29910a9e6</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:40Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:39:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/far-right-leader-le-pen-says-visible-policing-keeps-protests-smooth--be7e44d78d09.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/far-right-leader-le-pen-says-visible-policing-keeps-protests-smooth" /><summary type="html">Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. Our reporter Antonia Kerrigan has been following the latest developments.</summary></entry><entry><title>At least 1,300 people arrested in mass weekend protests across France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1dc72a658cc0fbe93c7f28d845edf897366223e03f079f1660a71ceec6a40f91</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T11:02:41Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-at-least-1-300-people-arrested-in-mass-weekend-protests-across-france--e24f12ed1515.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-at-least-1-300-people-arrested-in-mass-weekend-protests-across-france" /><summary type="html">At least 1,300 people were arrested on the sidelines of Saturday's protests across France and at least 50 officers and three protesters were hurt, the interior ministry said Sunday. The arrests occurred as clashes broke out on the margins of largely calm demonstrations in major cities over the high cost of living.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>Suspect in racist Paris shooting transferred to psychiatric unit, had drugs in his system</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5b734c790ca9db43be9274ec39a22ec526c397cb11a9ae6effdfaa2fd5098d24</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:47Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T10:36:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-suspect-in-racist-gun-attack-near-paris-transferred-to-psychiatric-unit--4ab912be23c8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261025-suspect-in-racist-gun-attack-near-paris-transferred-to-psychiatric-unit" /><summary type="html">A shooter suspected of killing one person and wounding five others on Friday in the town of Trappes, near Paris, has had his police custody suspended for a 48-hour psychiatric observation, anti-terror prosecutors said Sunday. The 39-year-old man is believed to have had racist motivations behind the gun attack as most of the victims had foreign origins.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>France: Some clashes erupted at end of protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3e261665c5a5b179ad598d84542b316529b27dcd32ec17d24bfdca47206b715a</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:49Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:35:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-some-clashes-erupted-at-end-of-protests--0c66a7dc9b2a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-some-clashes-erupted-at-end-of-protests" /><summary type="html">There were some clashes on Saturday, mainly in major cities and as demonstrations came to an end. French authorities say police made nearly 1,000 arrests nationwide. Here’s a report from our sister channel, France 2.</summary></entry><entry><title>More than 400.000 protest across France</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7d4ede7f52192d065bfc039fa58da3e7094b01ebbd312183387b88bfb1f485c9</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:52Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:32:21Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/more-than-400-000-protest-across-france--ddecf64571e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/more-than-400-000-protest-across-france" /><summary type="html">Saturday’s demonstrations across France were largely peaceful, easing fears of a repeat of the violence seen last weekend. Hundreds of thousands took to the streets to demand action on the rising cost of living and more funding for public services, with students, workers and retirees marching side by side. Many say their anger shows no sign of fading. The government put turnout at 410,000 nationwide, while the CGT union estimated the figure was almost twice as high. Here’s Jack Pownall with more.</summary></entry><entry><title>France’s far right gathers as Le Pen prepares her presidential campaign</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b5027092d593f6ba7b464f64073ed4ea9d56545471b6a1498c266cade2598ca4</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:55Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:31:02Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/france-s-far-right-gathers-as-le-pen-prepares-her-presidential-campaign--73e7ad8469e1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france-s-far-right-gathers-as-le-pen-prepares-her-presidential-campaign" /><summary type="html">Meanwhile, France’s far-right National Rally is holding the second day of its party congress in Orléans, south of Paris. Marine Le Pen is expected to officially launch her presidential campaign in a speech later this afternoon, while Jordan Bardella is being re-elected as party leader. On Saturday, a large counter-demonstration against the far right was held in the city. For more on what’s happening there, let’s cross to our reporter, Antonia Kerrigan.</summary></entry><entry><title>Hundreds of thousands march in France to protest against government</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2beaa309911f554d5bb6dd4a6fcf127c13e2533e4cb110d4786fe78883de2555</id><updated>2026-10-29T15:21:58Z</updated><published>2026-10-25T09:29:39Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/hundreds-of-thousands-march-in-france-to-protest-against-government--bc9fb991d30d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/hundreds-of-thousands-march-in-france-to-protest-against-government" /><summary type="html">Hundreds of thousands marched across France on Saturday protesting the rising cost of living and a lack of government resources for essential services like schools. While demonstrations in Paris started peacefully they ended with a heavy security response, with almost 1,000 arrested across the country. France 24 International Affairs Editor Oliver Farry shares further details.</summary></entry></feed>
